 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Switch action",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "One single-pole double-throw switch: D connects to S1 when IN is logic 0 and to S2 when IN is logic 1. Logic levels are 0.8 V max for a 0 and 2.4 V min for a 1. On a change of IN the closed switch opens before the other one closes (break-before-make)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Signal path",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "A closed switch conducts in both directions, so S and D may each be the input or the output. The signal may swing over the whole supply span, VSS to VDD (0 to VDD on a single supply), and an open switch blocks signals up to the supplies. RON stays nearly flat across that range, which keeps distortion low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supplies",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "VDD to VSS may be up to 44 V. The part is specified on ±15 V ± 10% and on a single +12 V ± 10% with VSS at 0 V, and still works on a single supply down to +5 V. VL is the +5 V logic supply. Each supply draws about 0.1 nA typical (1 µA max at 25 °C); power dissipation is under 35 µW."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Construction and compatibility",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "A monolithic CMOS switch on an enhanced LC2MOS process, giving low leakage, fast switching and low power. It drops into DG419 sockets pin for pin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timing definitions",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "tTRANSITION is measured from the 50% point of the IN edge to the 90% point of the switch turning on as it moves from one address state to the other. tD is the off or on time between the 90% points of the two switches during that change."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Off isolation and crosstalk",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Off isolation is the unwanted signal passing through an open channel; crosstalk is signal carried from one channel into the other by parasitic capacitance. Both are 80 dB or better typical at 1 MHz into 50 Ω, except T-version crosstalk at 70 dB."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "ESD-sensitive part. Charges up to 4000 V can collect unnoticed on people and test gear; the on-chip ESD protection does not prevent permanent damage or lost performance from a high-energy discharge."
  }
 ],
